---
/**
 * SiteMark — the initials, and the way home from anywhere.
 *
 * **AM** on the same 16-unit grid as the rest of the icon system, in one stroke
 * weight (1.5), butt caps, miter joins, `currentColor`.
 *
 * The A is the drawing that shipped before this, unchanged to the decimal. The
 * M is built to that A's *ink* metrics rather than its centreline ones, which
 * is the only way two letters drawn with a stroke can share a cap line:
 *
 * - stems flat at 1.7, so the A's mitred apex tip (1.492) stands above them by
 *   1.7% of cap height — the overshoot a point takes over a flat cap in any
 *   real face;
 * - stems flat on 14.1, which is where the A's foot centreline lands;
 * - the valley is the A's apex reflected — the same 55.6° included angle, the
 *   same 2.144 miter ratio — with its tip on 14.4498, the A's own lowest ink.
 *
 * Every diagonal in the mark runs at the A's leg angle, 27.8° off vertical.
 *
 * **Nothing in the mark touches anything.** The crossbar is 0.5 units off the
 * legs and the M is 0.5 units off the A, both measured at the tightest point —
 * for the letterfit that is the outer corner of the A's right foot, the one
 * place the A reaches furthest right. That is the rail's own grammar carried
 * into the glyph: the left edge of every page is a stack of free-floating
 * dashes, and this is a monogram held together by proximity rather than by
 * contact.
 *
 * `stroke-miterlimit="2.4"` on the M is the one attribute doing work. The A's
 * apex and the M's valley are 2.144 ratios and stay points; the M's two top
 * corners are 4.163 — a 3.1-unit spike out of the top of the box — and are cut
 * flat, which is what a geometric sans does at that junction.
 *
 * **`public/favicon.svg` is the A alone, and that is a measurement rather than
 * a preference.** Rasterised into 16x16, this lockup has *no fully inked pixel
 * at all*: its strokes land at 0.81 device pixels, peak ink 87%, against the A
 * alone's eighteen solid pixels at 100%. Every AM construction tried failed the
 * same way, and the only one that kept solid ink at 16px bought it by being
 * square and stopped reading as two letters. So the letters live at 20px and up
 * and the favicon keeps the A — which it can, because nothing in here touches:
 * the A is a component of the lockup, not a fragment of it.
 *
 * The same geometry is copied into `public/favicon.svg` (the A's two paths) and
 * `scripts/og.mjs` (all three). `lib/mark.test.mjs` compares them and fails if
 * any of them drifts.
 */
---

{/*
  No `aria-current` on home. The rail's Home dash already carries it, and two
  "current page" announcements for one destination is noise, not redundancy.
*/}
<a class="mark press" href="/" aria-label="Home">
  <svg
    class="mark__glyph"
    viewBox="0 0 29.75 16"
    aria-hidden="true"
    focusable="false"
  >
    <path
      d="M2.2 14.1 L8 3.1 L13.8 14.1"
      fill="none"
      stroke="currentColor"
      stroke-width="1.5"
      stroke-linejoin="miter"
      stroke-linecap="butt"></path>
    <path
      d="M5.9 10.4 H10.1"
      fill="none"
      stroke="currentColor"
      stroke-width="1.5"
      stroke-linecap="butt"></path>
    <path
      d="M15.713 14.1 L15.713 1.7 L21.588 12.842 L27.463 1.7 L27.463 14.1"
      fill="none"
      stroke="currentColor"
      stroke-width="1.5"
      stroke-linejoin="miter"
      stroke-linecap="butt"
      stroke-miterlimit="2.4"></path>
  </svg>
</a>

<style>
  /* Above the rail (40), below the mobile trigger (60) and everything modal:
     an open nav panel or palette should cover the mark, not fight it. */
  .mark {
    /* 20px below 900px, 24px above. The lockup is 2.06 ink widths where the A
       was one, and below 900px the mark has to stay inside the strip the shell
       clears for the vertical MENU label. At 375px that strip is 40px wide and
       the ink is 33.3px of it. */
    --mark-size: 20px;

    /* Optical, and the same correction the masthead makes with its -0.04em:
       line up the ink, not the box. The leftmost ink is still the outer corner
       of the A's left foot, 1.5366 units of 16 = 9.6% of the mark's height, so
       a box flush with the rail would put the letter 2px inboard of the dashes
       under it. Adding the M did not move this: it is measured off the A, and
       the A did not move. */
    margin-left: calc(var(--mark-size) * -0.096);

    position: fixed;
    top: clamp(1.25rem, 3.5vh, 2rem);
    /* Below 900px the shell clears `--page-pad + 2.5rem` on the left for the
       vertical MENU label. Sitting inside that strip is what keeps the mark
       out of the content column: the page scrolls past to the right of it
       rather than underneath it, and the MENU label is at the vertical middle,
       so the two never meet. */
    left: var(--page-pad);
    z-index: 50;
    display: block;
    color: var(--fg);
  }

  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .mark {
      --mark-size: 24px;
      /* The rail's own left edge. Mark and dashes share a column. */
      left: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.75rem);
    }
  }

  .mark:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  /* Height, not width: the box is 29.75 units wide against 16 tall, so the
     size token has to name the one of the two that has to match the rail. */
  .mark__glyph {
    display: block;
    height: var(--mark-size);
    width: auto;
  }

  /* A 40px hit area without a 40px layout box, the same `::before` trick the
     rail dashes use — the glyph stays exactly where `left` puts it. The
     vertical padding is derived rather than typed, so the 40px floor holds at
     both mark sizes. */
  .mark::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    inset: calc((40px - var(--mark-size)) / -2) -8px;
  }
</style>
